From b8c6c4b969aba02b119d0d4fb6ef1f5c6305b661 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:29:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Keep Inkling 2's lanes out of each other's way

A lane decoding while the other read a long prompt slowed to ~2.6
tok/s: the pool's adapt round took its lock alone every 4 steps and
waited for the prompt chunk holding it shared (seconds deep in a long
prompt), and the decode kernels queued behind the chunk's. Now the
round only runs when it gets the lock at once, and a lane's streams
are at the highest priority, the prompt executors' at the lowest:
decoding beside a 36K-token prompt read keeps half its speed (18 vs
36.8 tok/s; the prompt reads at 1517 tok/s meanwhile).

A client that sent a conversation again while its answer was still
being written had it read from the start on the other lane. Now a lane
keeps a snapshot at the end of each prompt it reads (each visit's conv
history and the last row's logits, a few MB), and a request whose
tokens start with a busy lane's snapshot copies that lane's state
instead: the cache cells from the RAM copy, the sliding-window rings
(while the busy lane has written fewer tokens since than they hold
past the window), the snapshot. A 15K-token conversation sent again
answered after 0.3 s instead of 10.7 (the copy 34 ms), the same text.

The second prompt executor (0009) is made after the lanes, their
sessions and snapshot buffers, so they always fit: it ran the cards
out of VRAM at start with two 512K sessions. It takes its ~1 GiB a
card from the coldest experts (about 44 at 2x512K), each card left at
the pool's reserve (--vram-reserve-mib) after it.

A lane taking another conversation threw away the one it held, and a
client's next turn matched what a lane held only up to the end of its
last prompt (the answer it sends back is rendered again, not the tokens
the model wrote, thinking and all), so most turns were read from the
start. Now a lane's snapshot at the end of each prompt also keeps its
sliding-window rings in pinned RAM: a turn that matches the last
prompt but not the answer rewinds the lane to it (the rings and conv
histories back, the VRAM window refilled from the RAM copy). A lane
taking another conversation parks the one it held in RAM
(--conversation-cache-mib, -slots, -min-tokens: its snapshot's cells,
rings and conv histories, oldest used out first), and a request that
continues a parked conversation has it copied back: three 9.5K-token
conversations on two lanes, the first back after 0.3 s (restored in
68 ms, 9 tokens read) instead of 5.7.

The Monitor and About tabs showed no experts in VRAM: the engine now
prints the INFO line the server reads (context, kv, the largest lane
window as kv_resident, expert_slots, experts, expert_cache_mib, the
conversation cache's settings) at start and before each DONE / BDONE,
as the pool moves experts. The park and restore lines use the
"conversation cache: ... parked=P bytes=B" form the Monitor's card
reads. --lookup-max-t reaches the lanes again.

A request matched a lane only when all the lane held started it, so
one that shared most of a conversation but not its end (a side request
with the same history, an edited last message) was read from token 0.
Now a prompt read keeps checkpoints (every 8K tokens to 64K, 16K
beyond, and at each prompt's end): each sliding-window visit's last
swa cells and each visit's conv history, ~170 MB, copied as each visit
of the chunk ends; the tiered caches' cells are in the RAM copy. A
request continues from the longest checkpoint that starts it, in any
lane or parked conversation (which keeps its checkpoints), and reads
the rest: the same state as reading it all. Restoring a parked
conversation no longer lets parking the lane's own drop it first.
